WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.429
Dear Fellow Scholars this is Two Minute

00:00:02.429 --> 00:00:04.420
papers with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.440
If we would like to see how digitally modeled

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.460
objects would look like in real life, we

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.620
would create a 3D model of the desired

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.990
scene, assign material models to the

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.330
objects within, and use a photorealistic

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.670
rendering algorithm to finish the job.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.920
It simulates rays of light that connect

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.930
the camera to the light sources in the scene,

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.020
and compute the flow of energy between them.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.099
Initially after a few rays we'll

00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:28.320
only have a rough idea on how the image

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.279
should look like,

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:30.929
therefore our initial results will

00:00:30.929 --> 00:00:33.540
contain a substantial amount of noise.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.520
We can get rid of this by simulating the

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.680
paths of millions and millions of rays

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.750
that will eventually clean up our image.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.180
This process: where a noisy image gets

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.250
clearer and clearer -- we call convergence,

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.989
and the problem is that this can take

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:48.989
excruciatingly long, even up to hours to

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:51.420
get a perfectly clear image.

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:54.480
With the simpler algorithms out there we

00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:56.420
generate these light paths randomly.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:58.640
This technique is called path tracing.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.120
However, in the scene that you see here, most

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.219
random paths can't connect the camera and

00:01:03.219 --> 00:01:05.019
the light source because this wall is in

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.020
the way -- obstructing many of them.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.820
Light paths like these don't contribute

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.899
anything to our calculations and are

00:01:10.899 --> 00:01:13.180
ultimately a waste of time and precious

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:16.000
resources. After generating hundreds of

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.399
random light paths we have found a path

00:01:18.399 --> 00:01:20.619
that finally connects the camera with

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:21.939
the light source without any

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:24.009
obstructions. When generating the next

00:01:24.009 --> 00:01:26.530
path it would be a crime not to use this

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.780
knowledge to our advantage. A technique

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.670
called "Metropolis Light Transport" will

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.500
make sure to use this valuable knowledge

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.080
and upon finding a bright light path it

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.149
will explore other paths that are nearby

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:39.459
to have the best shot at creating valid,

00:01:39.459 --> 00:01:41.830
unobstructed connections. If we have a

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:43.240
difficult scene at hand

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.220
Metropolis Light Transport gives us way

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:46.840
better results than traditional,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.490
completely random path sampling

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.860
techniques such as path tracing. Here are

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.020
some equal-time comparisons against path tracing

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.610
to show how big of a difference

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.890
this technique makes. An equal-time

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:58.869
comparison means that we save the output

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:00.879
of two algorithms that we ran for the

00:02:00.879 --> 00:02:03.069
same amount of time on the same scene

00:02:03.069 --> 00:02:06.260
and see which one performs better.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.180
This scene is extremely difficult in the

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.170
sense that the only source of light is

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:13.269
coming from the upper left and after the

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:15.370
light goes through multiple glass spheres

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:16.900
most of the light paths that we

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.940
generate will be invalid. As you can see

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.310
the random path tracer yields really

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.780
dreadful results.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.580
Well, if you can call a black image

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.379
a result that is. And as you can see

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:28.930
Metropolis Light Transport is extremely

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.700
useful in these cases.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.260
And here's the beautiful, completely

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.870
cleaned up, converged result. The lead

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.910
author of this technique, Eric Veach, won

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.100
a technical Oscar Award for his work,

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.840
one of which was Metropolis Light Transport.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.510
If you like the series please

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.430
click on that subscribe button to become

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.750
a Fellow Scholar.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.820
Thanks for watching. There are millions

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.860
of videos out there and you decided to

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.660
take your time with this one. That is

00:02:53.660 --> 00:03:00.970
amazing! Thank you and I'll see you next time!

